#First viewpoint analysis (societal lens)
From a societal viewpoint, a phone matters less for its features than for how it changes relationships, access and everyday behaviour. This section follows the shift from Nokia-style handsets to smartphones through three social questions: how people stay connected, who gains access, and what the costs are for wellbeing.
##Staying connected in the nokia era
mobile phone first changed social life by making people reachable wherever they were. In the late 1990s and early 2000s, Nokia-style handsets were cheap, durable and simple, so they spread quickly across age groups and countries. Calls and text messages let people arrange meetings at short notice, check in with family and keep friendships going without a shared place. Wei and Lo (2006) found that mobile phone use is linked to a stronger sense of social connectedness, because people use the device to maintain ties with family and friends and not only to handle practical matters. Text messaging was especially useful because it was cheap and private, which suited young people building their own social circles. Handsets also became personal objects, with changeable covers, ringtones and games, so the phone expressed identity as well as enabling contact. Through a societal lens, users were not passive: their habits turned a communication tool into a social practice.
###Access and Inclusion
Low-cost handsets mattered most where landlines were scarce. Aker and Mbiti (2010) show that in Africa mobile phones lowered the cost of finding and sharing information, helped traders and farmers coordinate and compare prices, and opened the way to mobile money services. For many households, a Nokia-era phone was their first telephone, so mobile communication became a shared social resource and not a privilege of the wealthy. The same authors caution that benefits depend on income, literacy and network coverage, and that evidence on long-term effects is still limited. A societal lens therefore treats access as a question of inclusion: mobile technology widened participation, but it did not remove existing inequalities.

